window.PRACTICE_SET_2 = {"questions":[{"chapter":1,"topic":"Defining success before delivery","section":"Defining success before delivery","pages":"15–18","stem":"Before a service-automation project starts, the sponsor proposes measuring only whether the software launches. Which additional measure most directly tests the intended business result?","options":["Whether the project has a complete list of technical activities","Whether average service-processing time decreases after adoption","Whether the project team uses the approved status-report template","Whether all project meetings occur on their planned dates"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Business results concern the value the project was intended to create. Processing-time improvement measures the service outcome; meetings, templates, and activity lists measure management practices or preparation. Set success criteria early and arrange to measure benefits after delivery.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-001","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Identifying the constrained objective","section":"Identifying the constrained objective","pages":"7–9","stem":"A required device shipment will arrive one week after the installation deadline. Its quantity, specification, and price are unchanged. Which project objective is directly threatened by the arrival date?","options":["Scope","Schedule","Cost","Product functionality"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The decisive detail is that the shipment arrives after the required installation date, directly threatening schedule. It may later create cost or other effects, but those are not established by the scenario. Distinguish a stated impact from a possible consequence.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-002","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Knowledge areas in use","section":"Knowledge areas in use","pages":"10–12","stem":"A manager identifies who needs a weekly dashboard, what information it should contain, and how it will be distributed. Which knowledge area most directly addresses this work?","options":["Project communications management","Project resource management","Project procurement management","Project quality management"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Communications management addresses the generation, distribution, storage, and use of project information. Resource management concerns people and physical resources; procurement concerns outside purchases; quality concerns meeting relevant requirements. The dashboard’s purpose here is information delivery.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-003","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Quality versus scope","section":"Quality versus scope","pages":"10–12","stem":"An approved report contains every requested field, but its totals are inaccurate. Which knowledge area most directly addresses whether the result meets the required standard of correctness?","options":["Project schedule management","Project quality management","Project stakeholder management","Project procurement management"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Including the agreed fields addresses scope coverage; calculating them correctly is a quality concern. Scope and quality interact, but the problem described is a deliverable that fails a correctness requirement.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-004","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Selecting appropriate tools","section":"Selecting appropriate tools","pages":"12–15","stem":"A sponsor wants to compare planned start and finish dates across activities at a glance. Which tool most directly serves that purpose?","options":["Weighted scoring model","Stakeholder analysis","Gantt chart","WBS dictionary"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A Gantt chart displays activities against time. A WBS dictionary describes components of scope, a weighted scoring model compares alternatives, and stakeholder analysis examines people or groups. Choose the tool that answers the requested question.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-005","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Core, growth, and venture investments","section":"Core, growth, and venture investments","pages":"23","stem":"An IT investment is necessary to keep essential business services operating, with no practical option to leave it unfunded. In the chapter’s portfolio example, which category best fits?","options":["Venture","Program","Core","Growth"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Core projects support running the business and the example treats their costs as nondiscretionary. Growth projects expand the business; venture projects transform it. Program describes coordinated related work, not one of these three investment categories.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-006","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Growth investments","section":"Growth investments","pages":"23","stem":"A retailer funds a version of its existing website in another language to reach additional customers and increase revenue. Which category best matches the chapter’s portfolio example?","options":["Operations","Growth","Core","Venture"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The textbook’s growth category includes projects intended to increase revenues, such as extending a website to another language market. This is a temporary expansion project; the fact that it modifies an existing system does not make it recurring operations.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-007","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Strategic portfolio decisions","section":"Strategic portfolio decisions","pages":"19–23","stem":"Every proposed project has a positive business case, but the company cannot fund them all. Which decision is primarily a portfolio-management responsibility?","options":["Choose a combination of investments that best serves strategy within available resources","Approve a completed work package against its acceptance criteria","Assign developers to the next task on one selected project","Resolve a defect in a selected project’s product"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Portfolio management selects and balances investments against strategic objectives, risk, and resource constraints. The other choices concern managing or delivering work within a particular project. A positive individual case does not eliminate the need to prioritize.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-008","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Project management office","section":"Project management office","pages":"32–34","stem":"An organizational unit maintains project templates, trains project managers, and reviews project documentation for consistency. What is the best match?","options":["Project management office","The project’s primary customer","A single project’s development team","Change control board for one project"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A PMO supports project management across an organization through functions such as standards, training, information, and coordination. Its exact authority varies. A change control board focuses on authorized change decisions, rather than automatically owning all these organizational services.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-009","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Leadership and management","section":"Leadership and management","pages":"28–29","stem":"A project manager articulates a compelling long-term direction and helps people commit to it. Which emphasis best distinguishes this behaviour from day-to-day management?","options":["Configuration management through version identification","Management through detailed tracking and control","Procurement through supplier selection","Leadership through vision and influence"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The chapter contrasts leadership’s vision, long-term direction, and inspiration with management’s attention to organizing and controlling specific work. Effective project managers need both; the distinction does not mean one role is unnecessary.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-010","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Transactional and transformational leadership","section":"Transactional and transformational leadership","pages":"28","stem":"A team leader promises specific rewards for achieving agreed targets and addresses exceptions when targets are missed. Which style is most directly illustrated?","options":["Transformational","Servant leadership","Laissez-faire","Transactional"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Transactional leadership emphasizes exchanges, rewards or consequences, and management by exception. Transformational leadership emphasizes inspiring and empowering change. Both can occur in practice, but the described mechanism is the exchange tied to performance.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-011","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Technical competence and delegation","section":"Technical competence and delegation","pages":"26–27","stem":"An experienced business manager is assigned to an IT project involving unfamiliar specialist technology. Which approach best fits the textbook’s description of an effective IT project manager?","options":["Concentrate entirely on financial reporting and leave technical implications unexamined","Treat the most senior programmer as the only stakeholder whose judgment matters","Take over all technical design work before delegating any responsibility","Develop enough understanding to ask good questions and rely on qualified specialists while coordinating the whole project"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The PM needs relevant technical understanding, business awareness, and people skills. They do not need to be the top technical expert, but must organize expertise, understand implications, and manage the overall project rather than ignoring technical or stakeholder concerns.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-012","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Professional ethics and conflicts","section":"Professional ethics and conflicts","pages":"36–37","stem":"A manager’s personal financial interest in a potential supplier should be disclosed through appropriate channels rather than hidden because the supplier appears qualified.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Fairness, honesty, and responsibility support identifying and addressing conflicts of interest. Apparent technical suitability does not remove a personal interest or justify concealing it.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-013","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Portfolio components","section":"Portfolio components","pages":"21–22","stem":"Every component in a portfolio must have a direct technical dependency on every other component.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Portfolios group investments to achieve strategic objectives; their components need not be directly related. Related projects managed together for additional benefits are characteristic of a program.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-014","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Uncertainty and estimates","section":"Uncertainty and estimates","pages":"6–9","stem":"Recognizing uncertainty is compatible with sound project management; an initial estimate is not a guarantee that no assumptions will change.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Uncertainty is a project attribute. Good management makes assumptions visible, improves estimates as information develops, and controls resulting changes rather than pretending that early knowledge is perfect.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-015","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":1,"topic":"Megaprojects and programs","section":"Megaprojects and programs","pages":"18–19","stem":"Calling a single very large undertaking a megaproject automatically establishes that it is a program.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A megaproject describes a very large project. A program coordinates related projects and other program activities to gain benefits unavailable through separate management. Size alone does not establish that structure.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-016","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Systems analysis","section":"Systems analysis","pages":"49–50","stem":"A team breaks a service system into components, identifies a problem, and compares alternative ways to solve it. Which aspect of the systems approach is most directly described?","options":["Portfolio balancing","Organizational culture","Systems management","Systems analysis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Systems analysis is a problem-solving approach that examines the system and alternatives. Systems management addresses business, organizational, and technological issues; systems philosophy supplies the holistic viewpoint. These terms are related but not interchangeable.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-017","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Business sphere","section":"Business sphere","pages":"49–50","stem":"A technically feasible tool would cost more to maintain than the value of the service improvement it creates. Which sphere most directly captures this concern?","options":["Business","Organization","Symbolic","Technology"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The business sphere addresses financial justification and contribution to business needs. A tool can be technologically feasible yet poorly justified economically. Symbolic is an organizational frame, not one of the three spheres.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-018","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Human resources frame","section":"Human resources frame","pages":"51–53","stem":"A manager notices that employees’ need for development is poorly matched to repetitive assignments, so the manager revises roles and training. Which frame most directly focuses on this fit?","options":["Political","Human resources","Structural","Symbolic"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The human resources frame emphasizes the relationship between organizational needs and people’s needs, skills, and motivation. Role changes can also involve structure, but the decisive concern here is the fit and well-being of people.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-019","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Symbolic frame","section":"Symbolic frame","pages":"51–53","stem":"A sponsor’s attendance at a project celebration signals that the work matters, even though no reporting relationships or budgets change. Which frame best explains this effect?","options":["Symbolic","Structural","Human resources","Political"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The symbolic frame concerns meaning, rituals, image, and shared interpretations. Attendance can communicate significance beyond the event’s formal administrative effect. A reporting-line change would instead point more directly to the structural frame.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-020","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Functional authority","section":"Functional authority","pages":"53–56","stem":"Specialists report to their department heads, and a project coordinator must negotiate for their time because the coordinator has little formal authority. Which arrangement best fits?","options":["Strong matrix","A dedicated project team with full PM control","Functional organization","Project-oriented organization"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A functional structure groups people by specialty and gives functional managers substantial control. Project-oriented structures and strong matrices generally give project managers more authority. Negotiation remains important when the project role has limited formal control.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-021","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Balanced versus strong matrix","section":"Balanced versus strong matrix","pages":"53–56","stem":"An organization shares project budget control between functional and project management and describes the PM’s authority as low to moderate. Which matrix type best fits the textbook’s table?","options":["Strong matrix","Weak matrix","Pure project-oriented structure","Balanced matrix"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The balanced matrix lies between weak and strong arrangements, with shared or mixed budget control and low-to-moderate PM authority. A strong matrix gives the PM more authority and budget control; a weak matrix favours functional management.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-022","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Organizational culture: member identity","section":"Organizational culture: member identity","pages":"57","stem":"Employees describe themselves primarily as members of the whole company rather than as members of their profession or department. Which cultural characteristic is this?","options":["Reward criteria","People focus","Member identity","Unit integration"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Member identity concerns identification with the organization as a whole rather than with a job or profession. Unit integration concerns coordination among units; it is related but does not specifically describe how employees identify themselves.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-023","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Organizational culture: open systems","section":"Organizational culture: open systems","pages":"58","stem":"A firm continually monitors competitor moves and regulatory changes and adapts its projects accordingly. Which cultural characteristic is most directly illustrated?","options":["Open-systems focus","Control","Conflict tolerance","Group emphasis"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Open-systems focus is the extent to which an organization monitors and responds to its external environment. Control concerns internal rules and supervision; conflict tolerance concerns airing disagreement; group emphasis concerns organizing work around teams.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-024","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Governance and decision rights","section":"Governance and decision rights","pages":"61–62","stem":"Executives clarify who may authorize major IT investments, who oversees their risks, and how technology decisions support business goals. What is the main concern?","options":["Systems prototyping","Activity duration estimating","IT governance","Sprint task estimation"],"correct":2,"explanation":"IT governance addresses authority and control over key IT activities and decisions. It is broader than creating a project schedule or selecting a development technique and helps connect technology investment with business objectives.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-025","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Project champions","section":"Project champions","pages":"60","stem":"A senior manager who is not the formal sponsor actively builds support and advocates for a project across departments. What role is this person serving?","options":["Work-package owner","Daily Scrum facilitator","Project champion","Configuration specialist"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A champion is a key advocate. The sponsor may serve as champion, but the chapter allows another senior manager to take the advocacy role. Do not assume these titles must refer to the same person.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-026","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Choosing a hybrid approach","section":"Choosing a hybrid approach","pages":"64–66","stem":"A project has stable facility-installation requirements but uncertain software features that need frequent user feedback. Which approach best fits the differing needs?","options":["Remove all overall milestones because some work is adaptive","Reclassify the facility work as operations solely to avoid combining approaches","Require the same detailed upfront plan for both because they belong to one project","Tailor a hybrid approach using more predictive planning for the stable work and adaptive delivery for the uncertain work"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A hybrid approach can combine development approaches to suit different work. The reason is the nature and uncertainty of requirements, not a belief that one method is always superior. Overall coordination and constraints still need management.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-027","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Kanban and workflow","section":"Kanban and workflow","pages":"75–76","stem":"A team makes work visible on a board and limits how many items can be in progress at once to expose bottlenecks. Which approach is most directly represented?","options":["A project portfolio charter","Kanban","Waterfall","A weighted scoring model"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Kanban uses visual workflow and work-in-progress limits to support flow. A board may also be used by a Scrum team, so the approaches can coexist; a board and WIP limits do not by themselves establish a particular sprint or Scrum role structure.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-028","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Culture within organizations","section":"Culture within organizations","pages":"57–58","stem":"Different departments can have different subcultures even when they belong to the same organization.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Organizational culture is not necessarily uniform across every unit. A PM should understand the actual groups involved rather than infer identical values and practices from a shared company name.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-029","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Means–ends orientation","section":"Means–ends orientation","pages":"58","stem":"An emphasis on the outcomes achieved rather than only the procedures used is an example of member identity.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"That distinction concerns means–ends orientation. Member identity instead concerns whether people identify with the whole organization rather than their job or profession.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-030","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Globalization and sourcing","section":"Globalization and sourcing","pages":"69–72","stem":"Operating across countries can introduce differences in time zones, culture, and communication even when no work is contracted to an outside company.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Globalization does not require outsourcing. Geographic and cultural coordination challenges can exist within one multinational organization; outsourcing specifically involves obtaining goods or services from an external source.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-031","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":2,"topic":"Agile values","section":"Agile values","pages":"73–75","stem":"Because Agile values individuals and interactions over processes and tools, it treats processes and tools as having no value.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The manifesto expresses relative priorities while recognizing value in the items on the right. It does not call for eliminating useful processes, tools, documentation, contracts, or planning.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-032","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Knowledge areas versus process groups","section":"Knowledge areas versus process groups","pages":"86–92","stem":"An instructor asks students to classify a process both by the subject it manages and by the kind of management work being performed. Which pair of frameworks serves these two purposes?","options":["Product life cycles; project phases","Project phases; organizational frames","Portfolio categories; organizational structures","Knowledge areas; process groups"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Knowledge areas classify subject matter such as scope or risk. Process groups classify work such as planning or monitoring and controlling. Table 3-1 maps the two dimensions; neither is simply a list of product-development phases.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-033","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Planning processes","section":"Planning processes","pages":"91–92","stem":"A manager documents how future stakeholder engagement will be planned and monitored. Which process belongs to planning rather than executing?","options":["Plan Stakeholder Engagement","Conduct Procurements","Manage Communications","Manage Stakeholder Engagement"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Plan Stakeholder Engagement establishes an approach and belongs to planning. Managing engagement, managing communications, and conducting procurements belong to executing in the ninth edition’s mapping.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-034","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Managing quality versus controlling quality","section":"Managing quality versus controlling quality","pages":"91–92","stem":"According to Table 3-1, which mapping is correct?","options":["Manage Quality is executing; Control Quality is monitoring and controlling","Both processes belong to initiating","Manage Quality is planning; Control Quality is closing","Both processes belong to closing"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The table distinguishes Manage Quality in executing from Control Quality in monitoring and controlling. A quality-related activity is not automatically assigned to a group merely because it uses the word quality; learn the named processes and their purposes.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-035","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Resource process mapping","section":"Resource process mapping","pages":"91–92","stem":"Which set contains only executing processes in project resource management?","options":["Acquire Resources, Control Resources, Plan Resource Management","Acquire Resources, Develop Team, Manage Team","Develop Team, Estimate Activity Resources, Control Resources","Plan Resource Management, Estimate Activity Resources, Control Resources"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Acquire Resources, Develop Team, and Manage Team are executing processes. Planning includes Plan Resource Management and Estimate Activity Resources; Control Resources is monitoring and controlling. The distinction concerns what is being done with resources.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-036","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Six Sigma methods","section":"Six Sigma methods","pages":"93–95","stem":"A company wants to improve an existing service process using Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, and Control. Which method is it applying?","options":["DMAIC","DMADV","RUP","Scrum"],"correct":0,"explanation":"DMAIC is the Six Sigma improvement sequence for an existing process. DMADV instead includes Design and Verify and is associated with designing a new product or process. RUP and Scrum are different frameworks.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-037","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Pre-initiation and authorization","section":"Pre-initiation and authorization","pages":"95–108","stem":"Before formally authorizing a project, executives clarify the business need, identify a sponsor, and assess whether the proposed work is realistic. How should these preparatory activities be understood in the JWD example?","options":["They belong exclusively to project closure","They are evidence that the project has already completed planning","They replace the need to develop a charter","They are pre-initiation work, not a sixth PMI process group"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The JWD discussion describes useful work before formal initiation, including constraints, sponsorship, and justification. These activities do not add a sixth process group or replace the charter and later planning.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-038","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"JWD: business objective","section":"JWD: business objective","pages":"95–101","stem":"In the JWD Consulting intranet case, which outcome most directly connects the site’s features to its business case?","options":["The largest possible number of web pages regardless of use","Completion of a database even if consultants cannot use the information","Lower consulting costs and increased business through sharing useful project-management knowledge","A choice of software platform independent of organizational goals"],"correct":2,"explanation":"JWD’s intranet is intended to create business value through reusable templates, tools, articles, and expert help. The case illustrates that a technical deliverable is a means to business results, not the only success criterion.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-039","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Kick-off meetings","section":"Kick-off meetings","pages":"108–109","stem":"A project’s kick-off produces a list of decisions and follow-up tasks. Which addition would most improve the usefulness of those meeting records?","options":["A statement that every open issue is already resolved","Only a count of the people who attended","An owner and due date for each action","A replacement for the approved project charter"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Action owners and due dates turn discussion into accountable follow-up. Meeting records support coordination, but do not automatically resolve issues or replace formal authorization.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-040","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"JWD: approved changes and reporting","section":"JWD: approved changes and reporting","pages":"118–129","stem":"JWD’s original budget was $140,000 and the sponsor later approved an additional $10,000. Which final-report statement is most transparent?","options":["Report both the original amount and the approved increase, with the reason and resulting budget","Omit the original amount because only the final amount may be discussed","Treat the increase as proof that the project produced no business value","State that no budget change occurred because the sponsor approved it"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Approval can justify a change without making the original estimate disappear. The case ends with $150,000; good reporting explains original versus final figures and the business rationale rather than concealing the difference or assuming a single measure defines success.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-041","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Scrum planning and execution","section":"Scrum planning and execution","pages":"131–133","stem":"A team selects sprint work and plans tasks, then spends the next days building and testing the increment. In the chapter’s teaching comparison, these activities primarily correspond to which process groups, respectively?","options":["Closing; planning","Initiating; closing","Monitoring and controlling; initiating","Planning; executing"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Selecting and planning work maps to planning; performing the work maps to executing. Scrum activities can also provide monitoring and feedback, so the mapping is a useful comparison rather than a claim that every event serves only one purpose.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-042","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Interpreting a burndown","section":"Interpreting a burndown","pages":"131–138","stem":"A sprint burndown shows 40 units of remaining work, while the ideal line shows 25. Which interpretation is best supported?","options":["The chart alone proves which individual caused the difference","More work remains than on the ideal trajectory; investigate progress, scope changes, and estimate changes","Exactly 15 units of money have been overspent","The team must have completed 40 units since the sprint began"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The vertical value represents work remaining, not spending or completed work. A gap from the ideal line is a signal to investigate; changed estimates or scope can matter, so the chart does not by itself establish the cause or personal fault.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-043","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Release versus sprint completion","section":"Release versus sprint completion","pages":"132–138","stem":"An agile team has a usable increment at the end of a sprint, but the organization plans a customer release after another sprint. Which statement is most appropriate?","options":["The product backlog must be empty before any increment can be usable","A potentially shippable increment can exist before the chosen public release date","The sprint cannot be complete unless customers receive a public release that day","The team must label incomplete work as done to fit the release plan"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The chapter distinguishes sprints, increments, and planned releases. Usable or potentially shippable work need not be publicly released immediately, and its existence does not require finishing every product-backlog item.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-044","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Process map interpretation","section":"Process map interpretation","pages":"91–92","stem":"An empty scope-management cell in the executing column means scope is irrelevant while the team builds deliverables.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An empty cell means the edition assigns no named process in that knowledge area to that group. Approved scope still guides execution; the table does not remove its relevance.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-045","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Planning process count","section":"Planning process count","pages":"91–92","stem":"Planning has more named processes than executing in the ninth edition’s 49-process mapping.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The table contains 24 planning and 10 executing processes. Process counts are different from effort or spending: executing typically consumes more project resources even though it has fewer named processes.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-046","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Planning documents","section":"Planning documents","pages":"110–116","stem":"A planning document becomes useful only after every uncertainty about the project has been eliminated.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Planning proceeds using the best available information, visible assumptions, and appropriate detail. Plans can be refined or changed through the relevant controls; waiting for perfect certainty would undermine coordination.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-047","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"JWD agile roles","section":"JWD agile roles","pages":"133–134","stem":"In the agile JWD case, Joe Fleming serves as product owner and Erica Bell as ScrumMaster.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"These are the roles used in the textbook’s comparison. The change in approach still preserves a business objective, stakeholders, and project-level coordination.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-048","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Daily Scrum","section":"Daily Scrum","pages":"131","stem":"The daily Scrum is intended to be a brief coordination meeting, with detailed problem solving normally handled separately as needed.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The chapter describes a short meeting to share progress, plans, and impediments. Identifying an obstacle during it does not mean every participant must stay for an extended technical problem-solving session.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-049","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":3,"topic":"Scrum completion evidence","section":"Scrum completion evidence","pages":"135–138","stem":"A story is necessarily complete once its estimated hours have been spent, even if it produces no working result.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Time spent is effort, not proof of completion. Inspect working results against the relevant criteria and record unfinished work honestly; otherwise progress reports and remaining-work estimates become misleading.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-050","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Project selection categories","section":"Project selection categories","pages":"160–161","stem":"A new external rule requires a company to change a reporting system by a specified date. Which selection category best captures the trigger?","options":["Directive","Discretionary preference","Opportunity","Unrelated portfolio benefit"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A directive is a new requirement imposed by management, government, or another relevant authority. Problems concern undesirable existing conditions; opportunities concern potential improvements. A compliance project may be necessary even without an attractive standalone financial return.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-051","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Need, funding, and will","section":"Need, funding, and will","pages":"160","stem":"An important proposed project has adequate funding, but senior leaders will not commit resources or support implementation. Which broad selection consideration is weakest?","options":["Calculation of the discount factor","Availability of funding","Will to make the project succeed","Identification of the need"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The chapter highlights need, funding, and will. Having a real need and an approved amount of money does not establish commitment to make the project succeed. These are separate conditions to assess.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-052","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Comparing investments using NPV","section":"Comparing investments using NPV","pages":"162–165","stem":"Two mutually exclusive, one-year proposals each cost $10,000 now. A returns $11,000 at year-end and B returns $12,100 then. At 10%, which has the higher NPV, considering only these cash flows?","options":["A: its NPV is $1,000, while B’s is $0","B: its NPV is $1,000, while A’s is $0","They tie because their initial costs are equal","B: its NPV is $2,100, while A’s is $1,000"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A: −10,000 + 11,000/1.10 = 0. B: −10,000 + 12,100/1.10 = 1,000. Equal initial costs do not imply equal value. The $1,000 and $2,100 undiscounted surpluses are not both NPVs; other selection factors are outside this calculation.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-053","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"NPV with continuing costs","section":"NPV with continuing costs","pages":"162–165","stem":"A project costs $8,000 at time zero. At the end of year 1 it generates $11,000 in benefits and incurs $2,200 in operating costs. At a 10% discount rate, what is NPV?","options":["$800","$0","−$2,200","$2,000"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The year-1 net cash flow is 11,000 − 2,200 = 8,800. Its present value is 8,800/1.10 = 8,000, so NPV = 8,000 − 8,000 = 0. Omitting continuing costs would overstate the project’s value.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-054","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Interpreting negative ROI","section":"Interpreting negative ROI","pages":"165–166","stem":"Discounted benefits total $72,000 and discounted costs total $90,000. What is ROI using the textbook formula?","options":["20%","−20%","80%","−25%"],"correct":1,"explanation":"ROI = (72,000 − 90,000)/90,000 = −18,000/90,000 = −0.20, or −20%. The cost is the denominator. Benefits divided by costs gives 80%, which is a different ratio.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-055","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Payback with unequal inflows","section":"Payback with unequal inflows","pages":"166–167","stem":"An investment costs $9,000 now. It returns $2,000, $4,000, and $5,000 at the ends of years 1, 2, and 3. Under undiscounted, year-end cash-flow assumptions, when is the investment recovered?","options":["At the end of year 1","At the end of year 2","At the end of year 3","Exactly 2.6 years after the investment"],"correct":2,"explanation":"After year 2 the cumulative inflows are $6,000, below $9,000. Recovery occurs when the $5,000 year-3 payment arrives. A 2.6-year answer would require an additional assumption that the year-3 inflow arrives uniformly, which this question excludes.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-056","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Weighted selection trade-offs","section":"Weighted selection trade-offs","pages":"167–169","stem":"A selection model weights reliability 60% and speed 40%. A scores 90 and 50; B scores 70 and 90. Higher scores are better. What ranking follows?","options":["B ranks higher: 78 versus A’s 74","They tie because their two scores average to the same result","A ranks higher: 90 versus B’s 70","A ranks higher: 78 versus B’s 74"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A = 0.60(90) + 0.40(50) = 74. B = 0.60(70) + 0.40(90) = 78. A stronger score on the most heavily weighted criterion does not automatically produce the highest overall weighted score.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-057","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Benefits management plan","section":"Benefits management plan","pages":"169–170","stem":"Management wants to specify when expected savings will be realized, how they will be measured, and who will own follow-up after delivery. Which document most directly addresses these matters?","options":["Activity list","Team charter","Benefits management plan","Configuration status report"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The benefits management plan explains how and when benefits will be delivered and managed. The business case justifies the investment, while the charter authorizes the project; neither label should be substituted automatically for this benefits-management purpose.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-058","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Enterprise environmental factors and assets","section":"Enterprise environmental factors and assets","pages":"170","stem":"Which pair correctly classifies an industry standard and a repository of lessons from the organization’s past projects?","options":["Project charter; team charter","Scope baseline; schedule baseline","Organizational process asset; enterprise environmental factor","Enterprise environmental factor; organizational process asset"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Industry standards are environmental influences on the project; historical lessons are reusable organizational process assets. Classify the actual item, not merely whether it is available to the project manager.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-059","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Assumption logs","section":"Assumption logs","pages":"170–173","stem":"A project depends on a vendor API being ready next month, but the vendor has not confirmed the date. What is the most appropriate treatment of this planning assumption?","options":["Omit it from records because documenting uncertainty would invalidate the charter","Move it into the completed-deliverables list","Treat it as an approved baseline change without further analysis","Record it, assign follow-up, and validate it rather than treating it as an established fact"],"correct":3,"explanation":"An assumption log makes uncertain planning premises visible so they can be checked and managed. A belief used for planning is not automatically a confirmed fact; if it proves false, impacts and responses need appropriate management.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-060","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Preventive action versus defect repair","section":"Preventive action versus defect repair","pages":"182–183","stem":"A team fixes an incorrect tax calculation in a deliverable. Separately, it trains a backup specialist to reduce the risk of a future staffing gap. What are these actions, respectively?","options":["Preventive action; defect repair","Defect repair; preventive action","Configuration identification; scope acceptance","Corrective action; project authorization"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Repairing a nonconforming product is defect repair. Acting to reduce a future negative consequence is preventive action. Corrective action specifically realigns future project performance with the management plan.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-061","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Performance data, information, and reports","section":"Performance data, information, and reports","pages":"177–184","stem":"A team records 12 completed tests, determines that progress is 3 tests behind plan, and sends a status summary with the implications. What is the best sequence of classifications?","options":["Work performance data; work performance information; work performance report","Scope baseline; change request; project charter","Work performance information; approved change; work performance data","Work performance report; work performance data; scope baseline"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Raw observations are data. Analyzing them against a plan produces information, and communicating the interpreted status and implications produces a report. A comparison or report does not itself authorize a baseline change.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-062","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Change requests and approval","section":"Change requests and approval","pages":"183–187","stem":"A process that produces a change request does not necessarily have authority to approve that request.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Requests can arise throughout the project. Integrated change control applies the agreed decision authority and evaluates cross-project impacts; creating the request is distinct from authorizing implementation.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-063","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Execution and approved changes","section":"Execution and approved changes","pages":"177–180, 184–187","stem":"Once a requested change is approved, the team should implement it through the appropriate project work and update affected plans or documents as authorized.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Approval must be followed by coordinated implementation and consistent records. Direct and Manage Project Work implements approved changes; a decision alone does not make the physical result or documents correct.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-064","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Plans and project documents","section":"Plans and project documents","pages":"174–176","stem":"The stakeholder register and issue log are simply alternative names for the integrated project management plan.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Table 4-2 distinguishes project documents from management-plan components. A register or log supports management, but neither alone integrates the subsidiary plans, baselines, and overall management approach.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-065","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Strategic selection","section":"Strategic selection","pages":"156–161","stem":"Projects should be selected solely in ascending order of duration, even when this puts low-priority projects ahead of high-priority needs.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Duration is one consideration, but strategic alignment, priority, need, resources, and other criteria matter. Completing short work first is not a universal selection rule that overrides high-priority business needs.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-066","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"Knowledge transfer","section":"Knowledge transfer","pages":"180–182","stem":"Uploading every document to a repository guarantees that difficult-to-express experience and judgment have been transferred to other people.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Written repositories support explicit knowledge. Tacit knowledge often needs interaction, mentoring, discussion, or observation; storing files does not guarantee either understanding or transfer of expertise.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-067","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":4,"topic":"After-project benefits","section":"After-project benefits","pages":"188–190","stem":"A delivery project may close while responsibility for measuring its longer-term business benefits continues in the organization.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Closing transfers the result and documents outcomes and follow-up. Some benefits emerge after delivery, so ownership and measurement must continue appropriately rather than assuming closure means every benefit has already occurred.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-068","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Product scope and project scope","section":"Product scope and project scope","pages":"202–204","stem":"A team distinguishes the booking features users will receive from the analysis, testing, training, and deployment work needed to provide them. Which terms describe these two ideas, respectively?","options":["Project scope; product scope","Requirements management; portfolio management","Scope baseline; project charter","Product scope; project scope"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Product scope describes features and functions of the product or service. Project scope describes the work needed to deliver the result, including supporting work such as training and deployment. The two are related but are not interchangeable.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-069","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Scope management planning","section":"Scope management planning","pages":"203–204","stem":"A team agrees on how the scope statement and WBS will be prepared, how deliverables will be accepted, and how scope changes will be controlled. What is it establishing?","options":["Accepted deliverables","Approved WBS dictionary","Completed scope statement","Scope management plan"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The scope management plan describes the approach for defining, developing, validating, and controlling scope. It is a plan for managing scope, not the detailed definition of the deliverables or evidence that they have already been accepted.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-070","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Requirements elicitation through observation","section":"Requirements elicitation through observation","pages":"206–209","stem":"Staff describe a standard procedure, but the manager suspects that actual work includes important exceptions they rarely mention. Which technique most directly reveals what they do in practice?","options":["A count of approved changes","A schedule backward pass","Discounted cash-flow analysis","Observation of the work"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Observing people performing the work can reveal tacit steps, exceptions, and practical needs that interviews or documents may miss. Other elicitation techniques can complement observation; the decisive need here is seeing actual practice.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-071","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Requirements workshops","section":"Requirements workshops","pages":"206–209","stem":"Several stakeholder groups use conflicting terminology and need to reach a shared understanding of requirements together. Which approach most directly supports facilitated discussion and agreement?","options":["A facilitated requirements workshop","An unreviewed list of assumptions","A private duration estimate prepared by one developer","A project buffer"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A workshop brings relevant stakeholders together to clarify meanings, resolve differences, and develop shared understanding. Other tools can support the work, but do not provide the collaborative elicitation setting described.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-072","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Requirements validation versus scope validation","section":"Requirements validation versus scope validation","pages":"205, 224–227","stem":"Before building a system, the team checks whether the written requirements accurately reflect users’ needs. Which description best fits this activity?","options":["Requirements validation, rather than formal acceptance of completed deliverables","Project closure, because user needs have been documented","Validate Scope, because the finished product is being accepted","Control Schedule, because requirements always specify activity dates"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Requirements validation checks the requirements themselves against the need. Validate Scope concerns formal acceptance of completed deliverables. The project stage and object being checked distinguish these similarly named ideas.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-073","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Using traceability during a change","section":"Using traceability during a change","pages":"208–209, 227–230","stem":"An approved requirement changes, and the manager needs to identify the related design components and tests that may need revision. Which existing tool provides the most direct starting point?","options":["Team attendance sheet","Project organization chart","A list of milestone symbols","Requirements traceability matrix"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Traceability links requirements to their origins and downstream work or verification evidence. Those links help identify possible impacts of a change. They support impact analysis but do not themselves authorize changes to the baseline.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-074","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Acceptance criteria","section":"Acceptance criteria","pages":"209–212","stem":"Which statement would provide the clearest acceptance criterion for an agreed search feature?","options":["Search work should appear somewhere in the WBS","The search feature should feel modern","For the approved test dataset, the returned results must match the expected results in every agreed acceptance test","Developers should spend sufficient time on search"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Acceptance criteria define observable conditions for accepting the result. The test-based statement supplies a checkable standard; time spent, a vague preference, or a WBS label does not by itself establish acceptable performance.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-075","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Top-down and bottom-up WBS development","section":"Top-down and bottom-up WBS development","pages":"219–222","stem":"One team starts with major deliverables and subdivides them. Another identifies detailed work and then groups it into larger components. Which approaches are these, respectively?","options":["Analogy; top-down","Mind mapping; guidelines","Top-down; bottom-up","Bottom-up; analogy"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Top-down moves from broad deliverables to detail. Bottom-up begins with detailed work and organizes it into larger components. Analogy adapts a similar project; guidelines use relevant standards; mind mapping organizes ideas through branches.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-076","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"WBS identifiers","section":"WBS identifiers","pages":"213–219","stem":"A WBS labels two components 2.1 and 2.2. What can be concluded from these identifiers alone?","options":["Component 2.1 must finish before 2.2 starts","They indicate positions in a hierarchy, not necessarily a scheduling dependency","Component 2.2 has twice the duration of component 2.1","Both components require exactly two people"],"correct":1,"explanation":"WBS codes identify hierarchy and structure. Dependencies, resources, and durations require additional information; interpreting numbering as a chronological rule can create an incorrect schedule.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-077","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Checking WBS completeness","section":"Checking WBS completeness","pages":"213–224","stem":"A WBS includes all software features but omits the promised user training and transition to support. What is the main problem?","options":["Training must always be excluded because it is a service","The WBS does not cover the full agreed project scope","The WBS is invalid solely because it is organized by product","The WBS has too many work packages"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The WBS must include the total project work, including agreed supporting deliverables and management activities. Product-based organization is allowed; the omission, rather than the organizing scheme itself, is the issue.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-078","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Handling nonacceptance","section":"Handling nonacceptance","pages":"224–227","stem":"During formal scope validation, the customer identifies an unmet agreed acceptance criterion. What is the most appropriate response?","options":["Close the entire project immediately because the customer attended","Record the deliverable as accepted because the review meeting occurred","Delete the criterion so no variance remains","Document the nonacceptance and needed action, and use the appropriate change or correction process"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Validation can identify a deliverable that is not accepted and lead to a change request or other appropriate corrective work. Holding a review is not the same as obtaining acceptance, and the team cannot silently remove agreed criteria.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-079","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Prioritizing requirements: ResNet lesson","section":"Prioritizing requirements: ResNet lesson","pages":"228–231","stem":"A sponsor cannot implement every requested feature within capacity. Which response best reflects the chapter’s ResNet lesson?","options":["Prioritize with stakeholders and deliver the most valuable feasible requirements","Promise all features now and postpone reporting the shortfall","Let each developer add whichever request is most interesting","Assume satisfaction depends only on implementing the highest possible number of requests"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The ResNet discussion shows the value of prioritization: implementing a subset can satisfy stakeholders when the right needs are met. Feature count alone is not value, and commitments should reflect capacity and agreed priorities.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-080","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Late requirements defects","section":"Late requirements defects","pages":"205–207","stem":"Finding a requirements defect late can require rework across design, implementation, and testing, rather than only editing the requirements document.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The chapter’s relative-cost discussion illustrates how downstream work depends on requirements. Its historical figures are not universal dollar costs, but the underlying lesson is that late discovery can multiply rework.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-081","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"WBS work packages and activities","section":"WBS work packages and activities","pages":"213–224","stem":"Every WBS work package must correspond to exactly one schedule activity.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A work package is the lowest WBS component, but it can require multiple activities to produce its deliverable. Scope decomposition and the level of detail needed for scheduling are related, not necessarily one-to-one.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-082","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"Unrequested additions","section":"Unrequested additions","pages":"227–230","stem":"An unrequested extra feature can create scope problems even when the developer believes it would be helpful.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Unapproved additions consume resources and may affect risk, testing, acceptance, or support. Perceived usefulness does not replace the need for agreed priorities and appropriate scope decisions.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-083","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":5,"topic":"WBS dictionary and approval","section":"WBS dictionary and approval","pages":"222–224, 227–230","stem":"Editing a WBS dictionary entry is sufficient by itself to authorize new work outside the approved scope baseline.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A dictionary describes scope components; it is not independent approval authority. A baseline change must follow the applicable change-control process before documents are updated to reflect the authorized work.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-084","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Schedule management plan versus schedule","section":"Schedule management plan versus schedule","pages":"243–246","stem":"A manager specifies the scheduling method, reporting frequency, units, and thresholds that trigger action. Which output is being developed?","options":["Final milestone list","Schedule network diagram","Schedule management plan","Activity list"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The schedule management plan establishes how the schedule will be developed and controlled. Dates and dependencies belong to the schedule or its supporting models; listing activities or milestones alone does not define the management approach.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-085","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Rolling wave planning","section":"Rolling wave planning","pages":"246–247","stem":"A team details the next month’s activities while leaving work six months away at a higher level until more is known. What is this called?","options":["Fast tracking","Critical chain buffering","Rolling wave planning","Scope validation"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Rolling wave planning elaborates near-term work in detail and future work progressively. It does not mean abandoning planning for later work or automatically overlapping activities; it matches detail to available information.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-086","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Finish-to-start lag","section":"Finish-to-start lag","pages":"245–252","stem":"A finishes at time 9. B has a finish-to-start dependency on A with a 3-day lag. Assuming no other constraints, what is B’s earliest start?","options":["Time 9","Time 12","Time 6","Time 3"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A positive lag adds waiting time to the relationship: 9 + 3 = 12. The calculation uses time boundaries and assumes the stated dependency is the only constraint. Subtracting the lag would instead allow overlap.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-087","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Finish-to-start lead","section":"Finish-to-start lead","pages":"245–252","stem":"A is expected to finish at time 9. A feasible finish-to-start relationship permits B to start with a 2-day lead, with no other constraints. What is B’s earliest start?","options":["Time 7","Time 2","Time 11","Time 9"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A lead permits the successor to start before the predecessor’s finish by the specified amount: 9 − 2 = 7. This is an authorized overlap in the model, not a two-day wait after completion.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-088","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Start-to-finish relationship","section":"Start-to-finish relationship","pages":"250–251","stem":"Let A be the replacement support shift starting and B be the old support shift ending. B must not finish until A starts. Which dependency links A to B?","options":["Start-to-start","Finish-to-finish","Start-to-finish","Finish-to-start"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Start-to-finish links the predecessor’s start to the successor’s finish: B cannot finish until A starts. These relationships are named by the linked events, not by the chronological wording of the scenario.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-089","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Finish-to-finish relationship","section":"Finish-to-finish relationship","pages":"250–251","stem":"A is testing and B is the final test report. Drafting B can begin before A ends, but B cannot finish until A finishes. Which relationship is explicitly required?","options":["Start-to-start","Finish-to-start","Finish-to-finish","Start-to-finish"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The constraint is between A’s finish and B’s finish, so it is finish-to-finish. It does not require waiting until testing ends to start drafting, which would be a stronger and different finish-to-start constraint.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-090","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Backward pass at a split","section":"Backward pass at a split","pages":"261–263","stem":"Activity X takes 4 days and has two zero-lag finish-to-start successors whose late starts are time 12 and time 15. What are X’s late finish and late start?","options":["Late finish 15; late start 11","Late finish 12; late start 8","Late finish 27; late start 23","Late finish 12; late start 16"],"correct":1,"explanation":"At a split, X must finish in time for both successors, so LF = min(12,15) = 12. Then LS = LF − duration = 12 − 4 = 8. Taking the later successor start would delay the other successor.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-091","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Calculating total float","section":"Calculating total float","pages":"261–263","stem":"An activity has ES = 5, EF = 9, LS = 8, and LF = 12. How much total float does it have?","options":["4 time units","7 time units","0 time units","3 time units"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Total float = LS − ES = 8 − 5 = 3, also LF − EF = 12 − 9 = 3. EF − ES = 4 measures the activity’s duration, not its float.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-092","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Recalculating a network after a delay","section":"Recalculating a network after a delay","pages":"259–265","stem":"A takes 2 days. B takes 6 days after A; C takes 4 days after A. D takes 3 days after both B and C. If C increases to 7 days while other durations stay the same, what is the new project duration?","options":["14 days","11 days","18 days","12 days"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Originally A–B–D = 2+6+3 = 11 and A–C–D = 2+4+3 = 9. After C increases, A–C–D = 2+7+3 = 12 and becomes controlling. The three-day overrun first uses C’s two days of float, then delays the project by one day.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-093","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Least-cost compression with two critical paths","section":"Least-cost compression with two critical paths","pages":"263–265","stem":"Both A–B–E and A–C–E take 10 days and are critical. A or E can each be shortened one day for $300; B and C can each be shortened one day for $100. Reductions are feasible, independent, and no other path controls. What is the cheapest way to shorten the project by one day?","options":["Shorten only C, costing $100","Shorten A, costing $300","Shorten both B and C, costing $200 total","Shorten only B, costing $100"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Both critical paths must be shortened. Reducing B and C by one day costs $200 and reduces both paths to 9 days. Reducing only one leaves the other at 10. Shortening shared A or E also works but costs more under the stated assumptions.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-094","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"PERT versus an unweighted mean","section":"PERT versus an unweighted mean","pages":"268–269","stem":"An activity has optimistic, most likely, and pessimistic estimates of 8, 11, and 20 days. What does the textbook’s PERT weighted-average formula give?","options":["13 days","12 days","14 days","11 days"],"correct":1,"explanation":"PERT = (8 + 4×11 + 20)/6 = 72/6 = 12 days. The simple average is (8+11+20)/3 = 13 days, so it is important to use the specified weighting rather than average the three values equally.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-095","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Feeding buffers","section":"Feeding buffers","pages":"265–268","stem":"A critical-chain schedule places time protection where a noncritical branch joins work on the critical chain. What is this protection called?","options":["Feeding buffer","Project buffer","Discretionary dependency","Activity attribute"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A feeding buffer protects critical-chain work from delays arriving through a noncritical branch. A project buffer protects the overall completion date at the end of the chain. Neither is a dependency type or simply an activity’s descriptive attribute.","type":"mcq","id":"TB9-S2-096","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Tracking Gantt charts","section":"Tracking Gantt charts","pages":"255–259","stem":"A Tracking Gantt view can compare baseline timing with actual or current progress, provided the relevant baseline and progress data exist.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The comparison depends on a saved baseline and reliable progress information. A polished chart cannot recover missing baseline data or make inaccurate status reports true.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-097","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Milestones and supporting work","section":"Milestones and supporting work","pages":"247, 257","stem":"Because a milestone normally has zero duration, the activities required to achieve that milestone require no time or effort.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The milestone marks the event, such as approval obtained. Preparing, reviewing, and obtaining that approval can require substantial work, represented by activities leading to the milestone.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-098","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Parkinson’s Law","section":"Parkinson’s Law","pages":"266–267","stem":"The idea that work tends to expand to fill the time allowed is Parkinson’s Law.","options":["True","False"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The chapter uses Parkinson’s Law when discussing task padding and critical-chain buffers. Murphy’s Law concerns things going wrong; the two labels address different ideas.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-099","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."},{"chapter":6,"topic":"Parametric and analogous estimation","section":"Parametric and analogous estimation","pages":"253–254","stem":"Estimating 180 installations at a credible rate of 15 installations per day is simply the same technique as copying the total duration of a vaguely similar project.","options":["True","False"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The rate-based estimate is parametric: 180/15 = 12 days under the rate’s assumptions. Analogous estimation draws on comparable work. Both need valid evidence, but they use it differently.","type":"tf","id":"TB9-S2-100","origin":"Original Set 2 practice; fictional examples unless a textbook case is explicitly named."}]};
